[{"category":"section","location":"#AstroUniverse","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"The AstroUniverse package provides celestial-body definitions, physical constants, SPICE ephemerides, and orientation models. Other packages such as AstroFrames, AstroProp, and AstroSolve use these to build coordinate systems, evaluate forces, and estimate trajectories.\n\nThe CelestialBody type holds a body's gravitational parameter, radius, flattening, and NAIF ID. The package includes the Sun, the Moon, all eight planets, and Pluto, and you can define your own bodies. Ephemeris functions such as translate and the lunar orientation models read SPICE kernels, which are downloaded on first use and cached for later sessions. Body orientation is a model set on the body. Earth orientation uses data published by the International Earth Rotation and Reference Systems Service (IERS).\n\nAstroUniverse defines the interface for corrections to Earth orientation. Precision corrections are available in the EpicycleEnterprise package.","title":"AstroUniverse"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-Start","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"Start by reading a built-in body's properties or defining a body of your own. Distances are in kilometers, and gravitational parameters are in km³/s².\n\nusing AstroUniverse\n\n# Read the constants of a built-in body\nearth.mu                    # km³/s²\nmars.equatorial_radius      # km\nvenus.naifid\n\n# Define a body\nphobos = CelestialBody(name = \"Phobos\", mu = 7.0875e-4, equatorial_radius = 13.0, naifid = 401)\n\nTo query an ephemeris or evaluate an orientation model, supply an AstroEpochs Time, which states its time scale. The first ephemeris call may download about 111 MB of kernel data.\n\nusing AstroEpochs, AstroUniverse\n\nt = Time(\"2020-01-01T00:00:00\", UTC(), ISOT())\n\n# The Moon's position relative to Earth, km\ntranslate(earth, moon, t)\n\n# The Moon's position and velocity relative to Earth, km and km/s\ntranslate_state(earth, moon, t)\n\n# Transform a state from ICRF to Mars-fixed axes\nbody_axes_rotation(orientation_model(mars), mars.naifid, t)","title":"Quick Start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Celestial-Bodies","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"The built-in bodies are sun, mercury, venus, earth, moon, mars, jupiter, saturn, uranus, neptune, and pluto.\n\nEach CelestialBody stores a name, gravitational parameter, equatorial radius, flattening, and NAIF ID. To create a body, provide name, mu, equatorial_radius, and naifid. Flattening defaults to zero, a sphere, so nothing of Earth's is assumed.\n\nYou can change a body's gravitational parameter with set_gravparam! or by assigning its mu field. The gravitational parameter and radius must be finite and positive, and flattening must be finite and in [0, 1). These checks apply when you create a body and whenever you change one of these fields.\n\nusing AstroUniverse\n\n# Read a body's value\nget_gravparam(moon)\n\n# Use a different value for the rest of the session\nset_gravparam!(moon, 4902.8)\n\nChanging a built-in body changes the shared object for the current Julia session, so other code using that body sees the new value.\n\nThe table lists the built-in constants and their sources.\n\nBody μ, km³/s² Equatorial radius, km μ source Shape source\nSun 1.3271244004127942e11 695700 gm_de440.tpc pck00011.tpc\nMercury 22031.868551400003 2440.53 gm_de440.tpc pck00011.tpc\nVenus 324858.592 6051.8 gm_de440.tpc pck00011.tpc\nEarth 398600.4418 6378.137 WGS84 WGS84\nMoon 4902.8001184575496 1737.4 gm_de440.tpc pck00011.tpc\nMars 42828.375815756102 3396.19 gm_de440.tpc, system pck00011.tpc\nJupiter 1.2671276409999998e8 71492 gm_de440.tpc, system pck00011.tpc\nSaturn 3.7940584841799997e7 60268 gm_de440.tpc, system pck00011.tpc\nUranus 5.7945563999999985e6 25559 gm_de440.tpc, system pck00011.tpc\nNeptune 6.8365271005803989e6 24764 gm_de440.tpc, system pck00011.tpc\nPluto 975.5 1188.3 gm_de440.tpc, system pck00011.tpc\n\nA body's flattening comes from the polar and equatorial radii of the same source. Earth's is 1/298.257223563.","title":"Celestial Bodies"},{"category":"section","location":"#Choosing-Gravitational-Parameters","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"The built-in gravitational parameters are set up for Earth orbits. Earth uses the WGS84 value. Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune, and Pluto use system values that include their moons, since from Earth a planet and its moons act as one mass. Two other kinds of analysis need different values.\n\nIf your analysis needs constants consistent with DE440, such as work centered on the solar system barycenter, use Earth's DE440 value.\n\nusing AstroUniverse\n\n# Earth's μ from DE440\nset_gravparam!(earth, 398600.43550702266)\n\nThis choice matters. Starting from the same state with point-mass forces from Earth and ten third bodies, moving Earth's μ from 398600.4418 to 398600.43551 changes the position after 7 days by 145 m for an orbit with a semimajor axis of 7000 km, and by 59 m at 42164 km. Choose one value and use it throughout an analysis.\n\nIf you model a planet's moons as separate bodies, as you usually would in orbit about that planet, use the planet's own value.\n\nusing AstroUniverse\n\n# Mars's own μ, without Phobos and Deimos\nset_gravparam!(mars, 42828.37362069909)\n\nBody Built-in μ, km³/s² Own μ, gm_de440.tpc, km³/s²\nEarth 398600.4418 398600.43550702266\nMars 42828.375815756102 42828.37362069909\nJupiter 1.2671276409999998e8 1.266865319003704e8\nSaturn 3.7940584841799997e7 3.793120623436167e7\nUranus 5.7945563999999985e6 5.793951256527211e6\nNeptune 6.8365271005803989e6 6.835103145462294e6\nPluto 975.5 869.6138177608748\n\nThe Sun, Mercury, Venus, and the Moon keep their built-in values in both cases.","title":"Choosing Gravitational Parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"#Ephemeris-Translation","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"translate(from, to, t) returns the position of to relative to from, and translate_state(from, to, t) returns its position and velocity. Results use ICRF axes, with position in kilometers and velocity in kilometers per second. They are geometric: no light-time or aberration correction is applied.\n\nSupply the epoch as an AstroEpochs Time, in any time scale. A bare Julian date is an error, because a number does not say which scale it is on.\n\nThe default ephemeris covers 1950 to 2100, and a date outside the loaded kernels' coverage raises an error from SPICE. A custom body needs a loaded kernel that carries its NAIF ID. The Uranus and Neptune centers differ from JPL Horizons by 60 km to 4190 km at the dates compared, as described under Validation.\n\nusing AstroEpochs, AstroUniverse\n\nt = Time(\"2020-01-01T00:00:00\", UTC(), ISOT())\n\n# Mars's position relative to Earth, km\ntranslate(earth, mars, t)\n\n# Mars's position and velocity relative to Earth, km and km/s\ntranslate_state(earth, mars, t)\n\nWhen querying several bodies at one epoch, convert the epoch once and reuse it, as in t_tdb = t.tdb. Both functions convert to TDB on each call otherwise. They are safe to call from several threads, and the SPICE queries run one at a time.","title":"Ephemeris Translation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Body-Orientation","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"An orientation model defines a body's rotating axes as a function of time, and each model is named for the publication that defines it. Except for Earth's, a model takes the epoch as an AstroEpochs Time and returns the 6×6 matrix that transforms position and velocity from ICRF to the body's axes, including the contribution from the axes' rotation.\n\nUse orientation_model(body) to read a body's model and set_orientation!(body, model) to change it. The selection is stored by NAIF ID and shared throughout the Julia session. For built-in models, set_orientation! checks that the model supports the body. Custom models are accepted without being evaluated, and a SPICE frame is checked when the rotation is evaluated.\n\nBody Default model Other included models\nSun, planets other than Earth, Pluto IAU2015() IAU1991() for Mars\nMoon LunarPA(), principal axes (DE440) LunarME(), mean-Earth axes\nEarth the frame theory, IAU2006() FK5()\n\nusing AstroEpochs, AstroUniverse\n\nt = Time(2458849.5, TDB(), JD())\n\n# Read Mars's model and its rotation\nmodel = orientation_model(mars)\nbody_axes_rotation(model, mars.naifid, t)\n\n# Use the mean-Earth axes for the Moon\nset_orientation!(moon, LunarME())\n\nEarth's orientation is the frame theory, so set_orientation!(earth, FK5()) and set_frame_theory!(FK5()) are the same call. Its rotation is the ITRF chain, which needs UT1 and TT as well as TDB. AstroFrames evaluates it, and its body_fixed_rotation(model, naifid, epoch) evaluates any model, Earth's included.\n\nSome data products, such as gravity fields, are defined in axes that differ from the body's default. Specify the orientation the data product requires rather than changing the body's default for unrelated calculations.\n\nA custom body can use a frame from a loaded SPICE kernel, SpiceOrientation(\"…\"), or a model written in Julia. A subtype of AbstractOrientationModel represents a way of orienting a body, and it implements one method. Writing the fields with a type parameter lets an estimator store dual numbers in them.\n\nusing AstroEpochs, AstroUniverse\n\nstruct SimpleSpin{T<:Real} <: AbstractOrientationModel\n    pole_ra::T      # deg\n    pole_dec::T     # deg\n    pm0::T          # deg at J2000\n    spin_rate::T    # deg/day\nend\n\nfunction AstroUniverse.body_axes_rotation(m::SimpleSpin, naifid, t::Time)\n    d = t.tdb.jd - 2451545.0\n    Ẇ = deg2rad(m.spin_rate)\n    return pole_axes_rotation(deg2rad(m.pole_ra), deg2rad(m.pole_dec),\n                              deg2rad(m.pm0) + Ẇ * d, zero(Ẇ), zero(Ẇ), Ẇ / 86_400)\nend\n\n# Set the model on a body\nbennu = CelestialBody(\"Bennu\", 4.892e-9, 0.2825, 0.0, 2101955)\nset_orientation!(bennu, SimpleSpin(85.46, -60.36, 89.6, 2011.145))\n\nOnce the model is set, coordinate systems and force models that read the body's orientation use it.","title":"Body Orientation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Earth-Orientation","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"The active frame theory selects the default Earth precession-nutation chain. IAU2006() is the default, and FK5() selects the classical IAU-76/80 chain.\n\nusing AstroUniverse\n\n# Read the active theory\nframe_theory()\n\n# Select FK5\nset_frame_theory!(FK5())\n\nEarth orientation parameters (EOP) describe measured variations in Earth's rotation: UT1−UTC, polar motion, length of day, and celestial-pole offsets. They enter every transformation to or from an Earth-fixed frame. IAU2006() uses the IERS IAU 2000A series and FK5() the IAU 1980 series. Each series has its own table, so both can be loaded in one session.\n\nA table loads on first use and stays in memory for the session. AstroUniverse reads it from a local cache, and downloads a new copy if the cached file is missing, unreadable, or a week old. It tries the IERS data center first, then the US Naval Observatory, which publishes the same series in a fixed-width format.\n\nA download replaces the cached file only after it passes validation, which includes a check that its UT1−UTC values reach today. If no source supplies usable data, AstroUniverse uses a readable cached copy and warns about its age. With no usable copy, the error names the sources and how to load a file by hand.\n\neop_refresh! downloads and installs the latest table immediately. It throws if no source supplies usable data, and leaves the cached file unchanged.\n\neop_load reads either format from a local file. It applies the format and finite-value checks a download gets and refuses a file of the other theory's series, but it does not require the file to reach today, so a historical file loads.\n\nFor repeatable results, load a saved file with eop_load or install a table with set_eop! before the first transformation, for every series your calculation uses. This prevents EOP downloads. For a run with no network at all, the SPICE kernels described below must also be in the cache.\n\nA finals file carries the rapid values of IERS Bulletin A and, for past dates, the final values of Bulletin B. eop_load reads Bulletin A by default. With bulletin = :B, it uses Bulletin B where available and Bulletin A elsewhere. Length of day and the uncertainties always come from Bulletin A.\n\n<!-- doc-fragment -->\n\n# Read the active theory's table, loaded on first use\neop()\n\n# Download the latest series now\neop_refresh!()\neop_refresh!(; theory = FK5())\n\n# Read a local file, CSV or fixed width, with no network\neop_load(\"finals2000A.all\"; theory = IAU2006())\neop_load(\"finals2000A.all\"; bulletin = :B)\n\n# Install a table already in hand\nset_eop!(table)\n\nOutside a table's span, eop holds the value at the boundary. A query outside the polar-motion span warns once per session. Length of day and the celestial-pole offsets can end earlier, and are held without a warning, so check their coverage if your analysis depends on them.\n\nAn AbstractEopCorrection adds terms the daily tables do not carry. A subtype implements eop_correction(model, jd_utc, jd_tt), and set_eop_corrections! installs a list of them. A custom correction is responsible for returning valid values in the documented units, because a non-finite value propagates into the computed Earth orientation.\n\nnote: Enterprise Feature\nIersSubdaily, the ocean-tide, libration, and zonal corrections, requires the EpicycleEnterprise package. The open package defines AbstractEopCorrection, the interface it plugs into.","title":"Earth Orientation"},{"category":"section","location":"#SPICE-Kernels","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"AstroUniverse reads ephemerides and lunar orientation from SPICE kernels. The default set is a leap-second kernel from NAIF, the 1950–2100 DE440 ephemeris published with Epicycle, and NAIF's lunar orientation kernels. They are stored between Julia sessions with Scratch.jl, in the folder get_spice_directory() returns.\n\nThe download happens at the first call that needs a kernel, directly or through a package that uses AstroUniverse, and is about 111 MB, most of it the ephemeris.\n\nDownloads from registered kernel sources are verified against their SHA-256 checksums, and a cached registered file is checked by size and fetched again if the size is wrong. A download from any other URL is not checked.\n\nusing AstroUniverse itself never reaches the network. Later sessions can use the cached kernels without downloading them again, as long as all the files are present at the right size. If a download fails, the error names each file, where it comes from, and the folder to place it in by hand.\n\nusing AstroUniverse\n\n# Find the stored kernels\nget_spice_directory()\ncached_spice_kernels()\n\n# List the kernels loaded in this session\nloaded_spice_kernels()\n\n# Download another kernel and load it\ndownload_spice_kernel(\n    \"de440s.bsp\",\n    \"https://naif.jpl.nasa.gov/pub/naif/generic_kernels/spk/planets/de440s.bsp\",\n)\nload_spice_kernel(\"de440s.bsp\")\n\nunload_spice_kernel and unload_all_spice_kernels remove kernels from the current session without deleting downloaded files. unload_all_spice_kernels also turns off automatic loading of the default set for the rest of the session, so afterward you download and load every kernel your calculation needs.\n\nKERNEL_SOURCES lists the kernels the package knows by name, for download_spice_kernel(\"pck00011.tpc\") and the like. The kernel pool belongs to the process, and a kernel loaded later takes precedence over earlier ones for the bodies and times it covers.","title":"SPICE Kernels"},{"category":"section","location":"#Texture-Maps","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"Texture maps for the Sun, Moon, and planets are distributed in the package's data directory, and Pluto has none. Earth's image is from the NASA Earth Observatory Blue Marble collection. The others are provided by Solar System Scope under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 license.","title":"Texture Maps"},{"category":"section","location":"#Conventions","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"Distances are in km, velocities in km/s, and gravitational parameters in km³/s².\nEphemeris queries and orientation models take AstroEpochs Time values and evaluate them in TDB. Earth's transformation chain also uses UT1 and TT.\nInertial axes are ICRF. SPICE labels them J2000, and they are not FK5 mean equator and equinox of J2000.\ntranslate(from, to, t) is the position of to relative to from.\nOrientation model angles are in radians and rates in rad/s when passed to pole_axes_rotation.\nPolar motion is in arcseconds, UT1−UTC in seconds, length of day in milliseconds, and celestial-pole offsets in milliarcseconds.\nBodies, orientation selection, frame theory, EOP corrections, and the SPICE kernel pool are shared across a Julia session.","title":"Conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"#Differentiability","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"IAU2015, IAU1991, and EOP values are differentiable with ForwardDiff, and tests compare the derivatives with central differences. A custom model written in Julia is differentiable when its code and field types carry derivative information, and installed EOP corrections need to do the same.\n\nCalls that read SPICE, which are translate, translate_state, LunarPA, LunarME, and SpiceOrientation, have no derivative through the date and raise an error when given one.\n\nusing AstroEpochs, AstroUniverse, ForwardDiff\n\n# How one element of Mars's rotation changes per day\nForwardDiff.derivative(2458849.5) do jd\n    body_axes_rotation(IAU2015(), mars.naifid, Time(jd, TDB(), JD()))[1, 1]\nend","title":"Differentiability"},{"category":"section","location":"#Validation","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"The test suite compares positions with GMAT R2022a at two stored cases, Sun to Earth in 2015 and Moon to Earth in 1995, within 1 part in 10¹⁰.\n\nBarycentric positions were compared once, outside the test suite, with JPL Horizons at 2020-01-01 TDB. Mercury, Venus, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Pluto, Earth, and the Sun agree to the 0.0001 km the comparison reported, and the Moon to 0.0017 km.\n\nThe Uranus and Neptune centers differ from JPL Horizons by 700 km and 240 km at 2020-01-01, by 360 km and 60 km at J2000, and for Uranus by 4190 km at 2050. The difference comes from how each source relates a planet's center to the solar system barycenter. Horizons follows the barycenter chain of its satellite solutions. The default kernel keeps DE440's barycenters, which agree with Horizons to the 0.001 km the comparison reported, and adds the offset to the center. The offsets themselves agree to 1.0 km and 0.12 km.\n\nThe default kernel was compared once with JPL's de440.bsp at 5000 epochs for each barycenter, the Sun, Mercury, Venus, the Moon, and Earth, and every difference is exactly zero. The tests check the Jupiter, Saturn, and Pluto center offsets against JPL Horizons within 0.01 km at 2020-01-01, and that every body resolves at both ends of the span.\n\ntranslate_state's velocity is compared with a central difference of position for the Moon, Mars, Jupiter, and Neptune, within 1e-7 km/s.\n\nIAU2015 is compared with NAIF's pck00011.tpc for eight bodies, the planets other than Earth and Pluto, at one epoch, within 1e-5 arcsec in rotation and 1 part in 10⁹ in rate. This checks the implementation of the same publication and does not establish how well the publication describes the body. IAU1991 is compared with NAIF's preliminary_pck00004.tpc for Mars at three dates, within 1e-3 arcsec. LunarPA and LunarME are equal to the named SPICE frames.\n\nEvery built-in constant is compared with the source in the table under Celestial Bodies. EOP values are compared with published IERS rows within 1e-12 of each field's units, and the fixed-width and CSV files give identical values at the dates tested.","title":"Validation"},{"category":"section","location":"#References","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"Acton, C. H. (1996), Ancillary data services of NASA's Navigation and Ancillary Information Facility, Planetary and Space Science 44, 65.\nArchinal, B. A., et al. (2018), Report of the IAU Working Group on Cartographic Coordinates and Rotational Elements: 2015, Celestial Mechanics and Dynamical Astronomy 130, 22.\nDavies, M. E., et al. (1992), Report of the IAU/IAG Working Group on Cartographic Coordinates and Rotational Elements of the Planets and Satellites: 1991, Celestial Mechanics and Dynamical Astronomy 53, 377.\nInternational Earth Rotation and Reference Systems Service, Earth orientation data, https://www.iers.org.\nNational Geospatial-Intelligence Agency (2014), Department of Defense World Geodetic System 1984, NGA.STND.00361.0.0WGS84.\nNAIF, generic kernels gm_de440.tpc and pck00011.tpc, https://naif.jpl.nasa.gov/pub/naif/generic_kernels/.\nPark, R. S., et al. (2021), The JPL Planetary and Lunar Ephemerides DE440 and DE441, The Astronomical Journal 161, 105.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"#API-Reference","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"type","location":"#AstroUniverse.AbstractEopCorrection","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"AbstractEopCorrection\n\nSupertype for a correction added to the daily EOP table at an epoch: the diurnal and semidiurnal ocean-tide terms, libration, the zonal-tide interpolation, or offsets estimated in a fit.\n\nA subtype implements\n\neop_correction(model, jd_utc, jd_tt) -> (x = ..., y = ..., ut1_utc = ..., lod = ...)\n\nreturning a NamedTuple with those four names: what to add to polar motion x and y in arcseconds, to UT1−UTC in seconds and to the length of day in milliseconds. jd_utc is the UTC Julian date as an ordinary date with every day 86400 s long, the date the IERS tables are indexed by; jd_tt is the TT Julian date, the argument of the tidal and libration series. Both may be dual numbers.\n\nThe models installed with set_eop_corrections! are evaluated at every epoch eop is asked for. A model whose terms are costly caches its own last value. The precision models are in the EpicycleEnterprise package.\n\nExample\n\nA constant pole offset, as a test of the interface:\n\nusing AstroUniverse\n\nstruct PoleShift <: AbstractEopCorrection\n    dx::Float64      # arcsec\nend\nAstroUniverse.eop_correction(m::PoleShift, jd_utc, jd_tt) = (x = m.dx, y = 0.0, ut1_utc = 0.0, lod = 0.0)\n\neop_correction(PoleShift(0.001), 2460000.5, 2460000.5008)\n\n# output\n(x = 0.001, y = 0.0, ut1_utc = 0.0, lod = 0.0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.AbstractEopCorrection"},{"category":"type","location":"#AstroUniverse.AbstractFrameTheory","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"AbstractFrameTheory\n\nSupertype for Earth precession-nutation models.\n\nThe theory names the model, and the model determines which EOP series it uses.\n\nSee also: FK5, IAU2006, frame_theory.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse\n\njulia> FK5() isa AbstractFrameTheory\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.AbstractFrameTheory"},{"category":"type","location":"#AstroUniverse.AbstractOrientationModel","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"AbstractOrientationModel\n\nSupertype for models describing how a body is oriented in space.\n\nInterface\n\nAn abstract type, so it has no fields of its own. A model must provide:\n\nbody_axes_rotation(model, naifid, t) — required. The rotation from inertial to body-fixed axes at that epoch.\norientation_parameters(model) — optional. Defaults to the model's fields, which is right for a model written as a plain struct.\nset_orientation_parameters(model, nt) — optional. Defaults to copying the model with those fields replaced.\n\nEstimation code can use the optional methods to read and update a model's parameters, and the defaults mean a plain struct needs no extra code for them.\n\nIncluded models, each named for the publication that defines it:\n\nIAU2015, the default for the Sun, the planets other than Earth, and Pluto; IAU1991 for data defined in the older Mars axes.\nLunarPA, the Moon's default, and LunarME.\nIAU2006 and FK5, the frame theories, which are Earth's models. Their rotation is the ITRF chain in AstroFrames, which needs UT1 and TT as well as TDB, so body_axes_rotation raises for them and points there. AstroFrames' body_fixed_rotation evaluates any model, these included.\nSpiceOrientation, for a body whose orientation comes from a kernel.\n\nNotes\n\nSet a model on a body with set_orientation!; read it back with orientation_model. Until a body has one, body-fixed axes for it raise and say so.\n\nA custom model covers a body with no included model, such as an asteroid, a comet or a newly visited moon. It also covers a body whose orientation is being estimated, since a kernel cannot carry derivative information.\n\nExample\n\nusing AstroEpochs, AstroUniverse\n\nstruct SimpleSpin{T<:Real} <: AbstractOrientationModel\n    pole_ra::T          # deg\n    pole_dec::T         # deg\n    pm0::T              # deg at J2000\n    spin_rate::T        # deg/day\nend\n\nfunction AstroUniverse.body_axes_rotation(m::SimpleSpin, naifid, t::Time)\n    d = t.tdb.jd - 2451545.0\n    Ẇ = deg2rad(m.spin_rate)\n    return pole_axes_rotation(deg2rad(m.pole_ra), deg2rad(m.pole_dec),\n                              deg2rad(m.pm0) + Ẇ * d,\n                              zero(Ẇ), zero(Ẇ), Ẇ / 86_400)\nend\n\nbennu = CelestialBody(\"Bennu\", 4.892e-9, 0.2825, 0.0, 2101955)\nset_orientation!(bennu, SimpleSpin(85.46, -60.36, 89.6, 2011.145))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.AbstractOrientationModel"},{"category":"type","location":"#AstroUniverse.CelestialBody","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"CelestialBody(name, mu, equatorial_radius, flattening, naifid, texture_file = \"\")\nCelestialBody(; name, mu, equatorial_radius, naifid, flattening = 0.0, texture_file = \"\")\n\nA celestial body and its physical constants.\n\nFields\n\nname::String\nmu::T: gravitational parameter, km³/s².\nequatorial_radius::T: km.\nflattening::T: geometric flattening, dimensionless, in [0, 1).\nnaifid::Int: NAIF body ID, which selects the body's ephemeris and orientation.\ntexture_file::String: path to a texture image for visualization, or an empty string.\n\nNotes\n\nThe numeric fields share one element type T, at least Float64. A BigFloat or a dual number in any of them promotes the others.\nmu and equatorial_radius must be finite and positive, and flattening must be finite and in [0, 1). These are checked when a value is set, in the constructor or afterward. The name and the texture path are not checked.\n\nExamples\n\nusing AstroUniverse\nphobos = CelestialBody(name = \"Phobos\", mu = 7.087e-4, equatorial_radius = 13.0, naifid = 401)\nprintln(phobos)\n\n# output\nCelestialBody(\"Phobos\", naifid = 401)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.CelestialBody"},{"category":"type","location":"#AstroUniverse.EopValues","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"EopValues\n\nEarth orientation at one epoch, as eop(theory, epoch) returns it: the daily table interpolated to the epoch plus every installed correction.\n\nFields\n\ntheory: the frame theory the values belong to, FK5() or IAU2006().\nx, y: polar motion, in arcseconds.\nut1_utc: UT1−UTC, in seconds.\nlod: excess length of day, in milliseconds.\ncelestial_pole: the IERS celestial pole offsets, in milliarcseconds: (δx, δy) for IAU2006(), (δΔψ, δΔϵ) for FK5(). No correction applies to them.\n\nExample\n\nusing AstroUniverse, AstroEpochs\neop(IAU2006(), Time(\"2024-01-01T00:00:00\", UTC(), ISOT()))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.EopValues"},{"category":"type","location":"#AstroUniverse.FK5","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"FK5()\n\nThe classical IAU-76 precession and IAU-80 nutation model. Uses the IERS IAU 1980 EOP series.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse\n\njulia> FK5() isa AbstractFrameTheory\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.FK5"},{"category":"type","location":"#AstroUniverse.IAU1991","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"IAU1991()\n\nThe orientation of the IAU working group's 1991 report (Davies et al., 1992). Implemented for Mars only. Other NAIF IDs raise an ArgumentError.\n\nFor Mars the pole and prime meridian are linear in time:\n\nα₀ = 317.681° − 0.108° T      δ₀ = 52.886° − 0.061° T      W = 176.868° + 350.8919830° d\n\nwith T in Julian centuries and d in days of TDB past J2000. These are the values NAIF's PCK of the 1991 report carries (preliminary_pck00004.tpc).\n\nNotes\n\nNot the default for any body. Use it where data are defined in these axes, such as the JGM85F01 Mars gravity field. IAU2015's Mars prime meridian is 0.236° from this one at J2000, about 14 km on the surface.\n\nDifferentiable in time.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse, AstroEpochs\n\njulia> size(body_axes_rotation(IAU1991(), 499, Time(2458849.5, TDB(), JD())))\n(6, 6)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.IAU1991"},{"category":"type","location":"#AstroUniverse.IAU2006","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"IAU2006()\n\nIAU 2006 precession with IAU 2000A nutation, using the chain based on the Celestial Intermediate Origin. This is the default. Uses the IERS IAU 2000A EOP series.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse\n\njulia> IAU2006() isa AbstractFrameTheory\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.IAU2006"},{"category":"type","location":"#AstroUniverse.IAU2015","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"IAU2015()\n\nThe orientation of the IAU working group's 2015 report for the Sun, the planets other than Earth, and Pluto: pole direction and prime meridian as polynomials in time, with the periodic terms where the report gives them (Archinal et al., 2018).\n\nThis is the default for those bodies, and it applies until set_orientation! is called. Its coefficients are fixed, so it has no parameters to estimate; solving for a pole needs a model that carries the pole as a field.\n\nFields\n\nNone. The coefficients are the published ones and are not settable.\n\nNotes\n\nDifferentiable in time, so a trajectory's derivatives pass through body-fixed axes for these bodies.\n\nCovers NAIF IDs 10, 199, 299, 499, 599, 699, 799, 899 and 999. Earth and the Moon have separate models: IAU2006 or FK5 for Earth and LunarPA or LunarME for the Moon.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse, AstroEpochs\n\njulia> orientation_model(jupiter)\nIAU2015()\n\njulia> size(body_axes_rotation(IAU2015(), 599, Time(2458849.5, TDB(), JD())))\n(6, 6)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.IAU2015"},{"category":"type","location":"#AstroUniverse.IAU2015Orientation","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"IAU2015Orientation{T<:Real}\n\nPole orientation and prime-meridian angle for a celestial body per IAU 2015 recommendations, plus their time derivatives.\n\nFields\n\nra_pole::T             — right ascension of the pole (α₀) [rad]\ndec_pole::T            — declination of the pole (δ₀) [rad]\nprime_meridian::T      — prime meridian angle (W) [rad]\nra_pole_rate::T        — α̇₀ [rad/s]\ndec_pole_rate::T       — δ̇₀ [rad/s]\nprime_meridian_rate::T — Ẇ [rad/s]\n\nNotes\n\nAll angles in radians; rates in rad/s.\nThe formulas are evaluated in TDB. iau2015_orientation takes an AstroEpochs Time and converts it.\nReference: Archinal et al., \"Report of the IAU Working Group on Cartographic Coordinates and Rotational Elements: 2015\" (Cel. Mech. Dyn. Astron. 130:22, 2018).\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroEpochs, AstroUniverse\n\njulia> iau2015_orientation(mars, Time(2451545.0, TDB(), JD())) isa IAU2015Orientation\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.IAU2015Orientation"},{"category":"type","location":"#AstroUniverse.KernelSource","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"KernelSource(filename, url, sha256, size, purpose)\n\nDownload metadata and expected contents for a registered SPICE kernel.\n\nA download is verified by SHA-256 before it is cached. A cached file is checked by size, which detects an incomplete file without hashing a hundred megabytes each time the package loads.\n\nFields\n\nfilename::String: Local kernel filename.\nurl::String: Download location.\nsha256::String: Expected SHA-256 checksum.\nsize::Int: Expected file size in bytes.\npurpose::String: Data supplied by the kernel.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse\n\njulia> kernel_source(\"naif0012.tls\") isa KernelSource\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.KernelSource"},{"category":"type","location":"#AstroUniverse.LunarME","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"LunarME()\n\nThe Moon's mean-Earth/polar axes, the axes of lunar maps and surface coordinates, as defined against DE440.\n\nNotes\n\nRead from SPICE by the versioned frame name MOON_ME_DE440_ME421, a fixed rotation from the DE440 principal axes that aligns with the DE421 mean-Earth frame. That is the frame the alias MOON_ME names in the DE440 frame kernel. It is rotated from LunarPA by about 104 arcseconds, which is up to about 875 m on the surface.\n\nNot differentiable, like every model read from SPICE; see SpiceOrientation.\n\nExample\n\nusing AstroEpochs, AstroUniverse\n\nsize(body_axes_rotation(LunarME(), 301, Time(2458849.5, TDB(), JD())))   # (6, 6)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.LunarME"},{"category":"type","location":"#AstroUniverse.LunarPA","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"LunarPA()\n\nThe Moon's principal axes from JPL's DE440 ephemeris, including physical libration. This is the Moon's default orientation model. Lunar gravity fields are defined in axes of this kind, though usually from an earlier ephemeris, so a field may require a different frame.\n\nNotes\n\nRead from SPICE by the versioned frame name MOON_PA_DE440, not the alias MOON_PA. Frame kernels all define MOON_PA, and whichever is loaded last decides what it means; the versioned name means the same frame whatever else is loaded. The kernels, moon_pa_de440_200625.bpc and moon_de440_250416.tf, are loaded with the other default kernels.\n\nFor a frame from another ephemeris, such as the DE421 principal axes some older fields use, load its kernels and use SpiceOrientation with its versioned name, SpiceOrientation(\"MOON_PA_DE421\").\n\nNot differentiable, like every model read from SPICE; see SpiceOrientation.\n\nExample\n\nusing AstroEpochs, AstroUniverse\n\nsize(body_axes_rotation(LunarPA(), 301, Time(2458849.5, TDB(), JD())))   # (6, 6)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.LunarPA"},{"category":"type","location":"#AstroUniverse.Mu","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"Mu <: AbstractParamTag\n\nTag identifying the gravitational parameter μ = GM on a CelestialBody.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse; Mu() isa Mu\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.Mu"},{"category":"type","location":"#AstroUniverse.SpiceOrientation","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"SpiceOrientation(frame_name)\n\nOrientation read from a loaded SPICE frame, such as \"IAU_MARS\" or \"MOON_PA_DE421\".\n\nUse this for a body whose orientation is published as a kernel. The kernel supplies periodic and libration terms without duplicating its coefficients.\n\nFields\n\nframe::String — the SPICE frame name, such as \"IAU_MARS\" or \"MOON_PA_DE421\". The kernels defining it must be loaded before the rotation is evaluated; a text PCK defines the IAU_<BODY> frames. Prefer a versioned name to an alias such as \"MOON_PA\", which means whatever the last frame kernel loaded says it means.\n\nExample\n\nusing AstroFrames, AstroUniverse\n\ndownload_spice_kernel(\"pck00011.tpc\")\nload_spice_kernel(\"pck00011.tpc\")\nset_orientation!(mars, SpiceOrientation(\"IAU_MARS\"))\n\ncs = CoordinateSystem(mars, CelestialBodyFixed())\n\nNotes\n\nNot differentiable. SPICE computes in double precision behind a C interface, so derivative information cannot pass through it. A body whose orientation is being estimated, or a trajectory whose gradient runs through body-fixed axes, needs a model written in Julia; see pole_axes_rotation. This model exposes no estimable orientation parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.SpiceOrientation"},{"category":"function","location":"#AstroUniverse.body_axes_rotation","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"body_axes_rotation(model, naifid, t::Time) -> SMatrix{6,6}\n\nRotation from inertial axes to the body-fixed axes of body naifid at the epoch t.\n\nThis is the one method an orientation model has to implement.\n\nArguments\n\nmodel — the orientation model, an AbstractOrientationModel.\nnaifid::Integer — NAIF ID of the body being oriented. Passed so one model type can serve several bodies. A model for one body may leave it unused.\nt::Time — epoch, in any time scale. Orientation models are defined in TDB, so a model reads t.tdb. A dual-number epoch carries derivatives through a model written in Julia.\n\nReturns\n\nA 6×6 matrix rotating a state from inertial axes into the body's rotating axes, as pole_axes_rotation returns.\n\nNotes\n\nUse pole_axes_rotation for a model expressed as pole angles, a prime-meridian angle, and their rates.\n\nEarth's frame theories are evaluated through AstroFrames, so this raises an error for IAU2006 and FK5 and points there.\n\nWrite it in plain Julia if the body's orientation is being estimated, or if a trajectory's derivatives run through these axes. A model that calls out to SPICE cannot carry derivative information; see SpiceOrientation.\n\nExample\n\nusing AstroEpochs, AstroUniverse\n\nstruct SimpleSpin{T<:Real} <: AbstractOrientationModel\n    pole_ra::T      # deg\n    pole_dec::T     # deg\n    pm0::T          # deg at J2000\n    spin_rate::T    # deg/day\nend\n\nfunction AstroUniverse.body_axes_rotation(m::SimpleSpin, naifid, t::Time)\n    d = t.tdb.jd - 2451545.0\n    Ẇ = deg2rad(m.spin_rate)\n    return pole_axes_rotation(deg2rad(m.pole_ra), deg2rad(m.pole_dec),\n                              deg2rad(m.pm0) + Ẇ * d, zero(Ẇ), zero(Ẇ), Ẇ / 86_400)\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.body_axes_rotation"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.cached_spice_kernels-Tuple{}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"cached_spice_kernels() -> Vector{String}\n\nReturn the cached SPICE kernel file names, sorted alphabetically without regard to case. These are the files available to load; loaded_spice_kernels gives the ones in use.\n\nReturns\n\nThe file names with a recognized kernel extension.\n\nExamples\n\nusing AstroUniverse\n\ncached_spice_kernels()\n\nSee also: download_spice_kernel, loaded_spice_kernels, get_spice_directory\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.cached_spice_kernels"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.download_spice_kernel-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"download_spice_kernel(filename::AbstractString, url::AbstractString)\n\nDownload a SPICE kernel from the given URL to the cache directory.\n\nThe kernel is cached in the AstroUniverse managed scratch directory and persists across Julia sessions. A file of that name already in the cache is reused; delete it to fetch a new one.\n\nWhen filename and url match a registered source, the download is verified against its checksum and a cached file of the wrong size is fetched again. Any other download is not checked.\n\nThis function does not load the kernel. Use load_spice_kernel after downloading.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing after the kernel is present in the cache.\n\nArguments\n\nfilename: Name of the kernel file (e.g., \"de440s.bsp\")\nurl: Full URL to download the kernel from\n\nExamples\n\nusing AstroUniverse\n\n# The short DE440, 1849 to 2150, about 32 MB\ndownload_spice_kernel(\"de440s.bsp\",\n    \"https://naif.jpl.nasa.gov/pub/naif/generic_kernels/spk/planets/de440s.bsp\")\n\n# Then load it\nload_spice_kernel(\"de440s.bsp\")\n\nAll kernels available at: https://naif.jpl.nasa.gov/pub/naif/generic_kernels/\n\nSee also: load_spice_kernel, get_spice_directory\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.download_spice_kernel"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.download_spice_kernel-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"download_spice_kernel(filename::AbstractString)\n\nDownload a registered SPICE kernel and verify its checksum.\n\nThe download location and expected checksum come from KERNEL_SOURCES, so no URL is required and a bad transfer is not cached. A file already in the cache is checked by size, and fetched again only if the size is wrong. Use the two-argument form for a kernel that is not registered.\n\nThe kernel is cached and not loaded. Call load_spice_kernel to use it.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing after the kernel is present in the cache.\n\nExamples\n\nusing AstroUniverse\n\n# The lunar frames need orientation data and the frames that go with it.\ndownload_spice_kernel(\"moon_pa_de440_200625.bpc\")\ndownload_spice_kernel(\"moon_de440_250416.tf\")\nload_spice_kernel(\"moon_pa_de440_200625.bpc\")\nload_spice_kernel(\"moon_de440_250416.tf\")\n\nSee also: load_spice_kernel, kernel_source\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.download_spice_kernel"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.eop-Tuple{AbstractFrameTheory, Real, Real}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"eop(theory::AbstractFrameTheory, epoch::Time) -> EopValues\neop(epoch::Time) -> EopValues\neop(theory::AbstractFrameTheory, jd_utc::Real, jd_tt::Real) -> EopValues\n\nEarth orientation at epoch under theory, or under the active theory: the daily table read at the epoch, with every correction installed by set_eop_corrections! added to polar motion, UT1−UTC and the length of day.\n\nThis is the one place Earth orientation at an epoch comes from. The Earth frame chain, ground stations and Earth gravity all read it, so a correction installed here reaches all of them.\n\nArguments\n\ntheory: FK5() or IAU2006(), which selects the table.\nepoch: an AstroEpochs.Time.\njd_utc, jd_tt: the epoch as a UTC Julian date with every day 86400 s long, the date the tables are indexed by, and as a TT Julian date. This is the form the frame chain calls with, having derived the scales once; a Time is the form to use otherwise.\n\nReturns\n\nAn EopValues. The numeric type follows the epoch and the corrections, so a dual-number date or an estimated offset gives dual-number values.\n\nExample\n\nusing AstroUniverse, AstroEpochs\nv = eop(IAU2006(), Time(\"2024-01-01T00:00:00\", UTC(), ISOT()))\nv.x, v.y          # arcsec\nv.ut1_utc         # s\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.eop"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.eop-Tuple{AbstractFrameTheory}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"eop(theory::AbstractFrameTheory) -> EopIau1980 | EopIau2000A\neop() -> EopIau1980 | EopIau2000A\n\nEOP table for theory, or for the active theory if none is given.\n\nEach theory has a separate table that loads on first use and stays in memory for the session, so asking for one never fetches the other. The table is read from the cached IERS file, which is downloaded when it is missing or a week old. Each series has more than one source, IERS first and then the US Naval Observatory. When none can be reached a cached copy is used, with a warning giving its age.\n\nThread-safe.\n\nReturns\n\nAn EopIau1980 table for FK5() or an EopIau2000A table for IAU2006().\n\nExample\n\nusing AstroUniverse\n\ntable = eop(IAU2006())\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.eop"},{"category":"function","location":"#AstroUniverse.eop_correction","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"eop_correction(model::AbstractEopCorrection, jd_utc, jd_tt) -> NamedTuple\n\nWhat model adds to the daily EOP table at an epoch; see AbstractEopCorrection for the contract a model implements.\n\nReturns\n\nA NamedTuple with the names x, y, ut1_utc and lod.\n\nExample\n\nusing AstroUniverse\n\nstruct NoShift <: AbstractEopCorrection end\nAstroUniverse.eop_correction(::NoShift, jd_utc, jd_tt) = (x = 0.0, y = 0.0, ut1_utc = 0.0, lod = 0.0)\n\neop_correction(NoShift(), 2460000.5, 2460000.5008).x\n\n# output\n0.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.eop_correction"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.eop_corrections-Tuple{}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"eop_corrections() -> Vector{AbstractEopCorrection}\n\nThe corrections added to the daily EOP table in this session, in the order they are applied. Empty by default, so Earth orientation is the table alone.\n\nReturns\n\nThe installed vector of corrections.\n\nExample\n\nusing AstroUniverse\n\neop_corrections()\n\n# output\nAbstractEopCorrection[]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.eop_corrections"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.eop_load-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"eop_load(path::AbstractString; theory = frame_theory(), bulletin = :A) -> table\n\nRead a local IERS EOP file and install its table for theory. Does not use the network.\n\nThe file gets the format and finite-value checks a download gets. It does not have to reach today, so a historical file loads.\n\nA finals file carries two sets of values on each line: the rapid values of IERS Bulletin A, and for past dates the final values of Bulletin B. bulletin = :A reads the rapid values throughout. bulletin = :B reads the final polar motion, UT1−UTC and celestial pole offsets wherever Bulletin B has them and the rapid values after. Bulletin B has no length of day, so LOD and the uncertainties are Bulletin A's either way.\n\nArguments\n\npath: an IERS finals file, either the CSV the IERS data centre publishes (finals2000A.all.csv, finals.all.csv) or the fixed-width file the US Naval Observatory publishes (finals2000A.all, finals.all). The format is read from the file.\ntheory: the theory the file is for. A finals2000A file carries the IAU 2000A offsets IAU2006 needs, a finals file the IAU 1980 corrections FK5 needs, and a file of the other series is refused.\nbulletin: :A for the rapid values, :B for the final values where published.\n\nReturns\n\nThe parsed and installed EOP table.\n\nNotes\n\nA table's columns do not end together. Past its polar motion and UT1−UTC, the end values are held with a warning. LOD and the celestial pole offsets can end earlier and are held without one.\n\nExample\n\ntable = eop_load(\"finals2000A.all.csv\"; theory = IAU2006(), bulletin = :B)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.eop_load"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.eop_refresh!-Tuple{}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"eop_refresh!(; theory = frame_theory()) -> table\n\nDownload the latest EOP series for theory now, bypassing the freshness check, and install it in that theory's slot.\n\nThe sources are tried in order, IERS first and then the US Naval Observatory. Unlike the refresh eop makes when the cache is a week old, this throws an error if all sources fail. The existing cached file is preserved.\n\nReturns\n\nThe downloaded and installed EOP table.\n\nExample\n\nusing AstroUniverse\n\ntable = eop_refresh!(; theory = IAU2006())\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.eop_refresh!"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.frame_theory-Tuple{}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"frame_theory() -> AbstractFrameTheory\n\nReturn the active Earth precession-nutation theory. The default is IAU2006.\n\nThis selects the default chain when a transform's endpoints do not name a branch. It does not restrict which EOP data is available, since both series can be loaded at once (see eop).\n\nReturns\n\nThe active AbstractFrameTheory.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse\n\njulia> frame_theory() isa AbstractFrameTheory\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.frame_theory"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.get_gravparam-Tuple{CelestialBody}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"get_gravparam(body)\n\nReturn the body’s gravitational parameter μ.\n\nReturns\n\nThe gravitational parameter in km^3/s^2.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse; get_gravparam(earth) == earth.mu\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.get_gravparam"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.get_spice_directory-Tuple{}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"get_spice_directory()\n\nReturn the path to the AstroUniverse SPICE kernel cache directory.\n\nThis directory persists across Julia sessions and is managed by Scratch.jl. Kernel files may also be placed here manually.\n\nReturns\n\nThe absolute path to the managed kernel directory.\n\nExamples\n\nusing AstroUniverse\n\ndir = get_spice_directory()\nprintln(\"SPICE kernels cached at: \", dir)\n\n# Manually copy a kernel file to the cache\n# cp(\"my_kernel.bsp\", joinpath(dir, \"my_kernel.bsp\"))\n# Then load it\n# load_spice_kernel(\"my_kernel.bsp\")\n\nSee also: download_spice_kernel, cached_spice_kernels\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.get_spice_directory"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.has_orientation_model-Tuple{Any}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"has_orientation_model(body) -> Bool\n\nReturn whether a body has an orientation model set or a built-in default.\n\nArguments\n\nbody — a body, or a NAIF ID.\n\nReturns\n\ntrue if a model is set or a built-in default applies, false otherwise.\n\nNotes\n\nThis checks that a model is available. It does not evaluate the model, check that SPICE kernels are loaded, or check coverage at an epoch. Use orientation_model to retrieve the model.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse\n\njulia> has_orientation_model(mars), has_orientation_model(earth)\n(true, true)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.has_orientation_model"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.iau2015_orientation-Tuple{CelestialBody, Time}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"iau2015_orientation(body, t::Time) → IAU2015Orientation\n\nReturn the IAU 2015 orientation parameters for body at the epoch t.\n\nSupported bodies: Sun, Mercury, Venus, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune, Pluto.\n\nNot supported\n\nEarth (naifid 399). Earth rotation is model-family dependent (FK5 IAU 1980 vs IAU 2006/2010) and requires EOP corrections; the IAU 2015 planet-rotation format is not the right primitive. Use the Earth-frame edges in AstroFrames (ICRS ↔ GCRS ↔ CIRS ↔ TIRS ↔ ITRS, or the FK5 chain).\nMoon (naifid 301). The IAU stopped publishing a simplified lunar rotation model; production lunar work uses SPICE PCK files with full physical librations. Use LunarPA or LunarME, or AstroFrames' MoonPA and MoonME axes.\n\nArguments\n\nbody: a CelestialBody, or a NAIF ID.\nt::Time: epoch, in any time scale. The polynomials are evaluated in TDB.\n\nErrors\n\nThrows ArgumentError for Earth and Moon (see above) and for any other body without a registered IAU 2015 implementation.\n\nReference\n\nArchinal et al., \"Report of the IAU Working Group on Cartographic Coordinates and Rotational Elements: 2015\" (Cel. Mech. Dyn. Astron. 130:22, 2018).\n\nReturns\n\nAn IAU2015Orientation containing pole, prime-meridian, and rate values.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse, AstroEpochs\n\njulia> iau2015_orientation(mars, Time(2451545.0, TDB(), JD())) isa IAU2015Orientation\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.iau2015_orientation"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.kernel_source-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"kernel_source(filename) -> Union{KernelSource,Nothing}\n\nReturn the registered source for filename, or nothing if the filename is not registered.\n\nReturns\n\nA KernelSource when filename is registered, otherwise nothing.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse\n\njulia> kernel_source(\"naif0012.tls\").purpose isa String\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.kernel_source"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.load_spice_kernel-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"load_spice_kernel(filename::AbstractString)\n\nLoad a cached kernel into the process-wide SPICE kernel pool.\n\nThe kernel file must already exist in the cache; use download_spice_kernel first if needed.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing after the kernel is loaded or found already loaded.\n\nArguments\n\nfilename: Name of the kernel file in the cache (e.g., \"de440s.bsp\")\n\nExamples\n\nusing AstroUniverse\n\n# Download then load\ndownload_spice_kernel(\"de440s.bsp\",\n    \"https://naif.jpl.nasa.gov/pub/naif/generic_kernels/spk/planets/de440s.bsp\")\nload_spice_kernel(\"de440s.bsp\")\n\n# Or just load if already downloaded\nload_spice_kernel(\"naif0012.tls\")  # Default kernel\n\nSee also: download_spice_kernel, unload_spice_kernel, unload_all_spice_kernels\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.load_spice_kernel"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.loaded_spice_kernels-Tuple{}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"loaded_spice_kernels() -> Vector{String}\n\nReturn the file names of the kernels in the SPICE kernel pool, in load order. A kernel loaded twice appears twice. cached_spice_kernels gives the files on disk.\n\nReturns\n\nThe file names.\n\nExamples\n\nusing AstroUniverse\n\n\"naif0012.tls\" in loaded_spice_kernels()\n\nSee also: load_spice_kernel, unload_spice_kernel, cached_spice_kernels\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.loaded_spice_kernels"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.orientation_model-Tuple{Any}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"orientation_model(body) -> AbstractOrientationModel\n\nReturn the orientation model set on a body, or its built-in default. Throws an ArgumentError if the body has neither.\n\nArguments\n\nbody — a body, or a NAIF ID.\n\nReturns\n\nThe body's AbstractOrientationModel — what was set with set_orientation!, or the built-in default for a body that has one.\n\nNotes\n\nThe defaults:\n\nEarth: the frame theory, frame_theory(), IAU2006() unless set otherwise. Its rotation is the ITRF chain, which AstroFrames evaluates.\nThe Moon: LunarPA, the principal axes, which is what dynamics and gravity use. Maps use LunarME; set it if body-fixed coordinates should be mean-Earth.\nThe Sun, the other planets and Pluto: IAU2015.\n\nUse has_orientation_model to ask without raising an error.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse\n\njulia> orientation_model(mars)\nIAU2015()\n\njulia> orientation_model(moon)\nLunarPA()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.orientation_model"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.orientation_parameters-Tuple{AbstractOrientationModel}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"orientation_parameters(model_or_body) -> NamedTuple\n\nReturn the orientation parameters that can be estimated, by name.\n\nArguments\n\nmodel_or_body — an AbstractOrientationModel, or a body, in which case its currently set model is read.\n\nReturns\n\nA NamedTuple of parameter names and their current values. Units follow the model's documented convention.\n\nNotes\n\nDefaults to the model's fields, so a model written as a plain struct needs no extra code. A model with fields that are not parameters should override this. A model with no estimable parameters, such as an included polynomial model or a SPICE frame, returns an empty NamedTuple.\n\nEstimation code reads the names here and writes back through set_orientation_parameters!. This package does not track which of them are being solved for.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse\n\njulia> struct Spin <: AbstractOrientationModel\n           pole_ra::Float64\n           pole_dec::Float64\n       end\n\njulia> orientation_parameters(Spin(85.46, -60.36))\n(pole_ra = 85.46, pole_dec = -60.36)\n\njulia> orientation_parameters(IAU2015())\nNamedTuple()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.orientation_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.orientation_parameters-Tuple{CelestialBody}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"orientation_parameters(body) -> NamedTuple\n\nThe orientation parameters of body that can be estimated, by name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.orientation_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.pole_axes_rotation-NTuple{6, Real}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"pole_axes_rotation(α₀, δ₀, W, α̇₀, δ̇₀, Ẇ) -> SMatrix{6,6}\n\nBuild the 6×6 state rotation from inertial axes to a body's rotating axes, given where its north pole points and how far its prime meridian has turned.\n\nArguments\n\nα₀, δ₀ — right ascension and declination of the body's north pole in inertial axes, in radians.\nW — angle from the node of the body's equator on the inertial equator to the body's prime meridian, in radians.\nα̇₀, δ̇₀, Ẇ — their rates, in radians per second. Convert a rate in degrees per day with deg2rad(rate) / 86_400.\n\nIt suits a model of the usual shape, a pole direction and a spin about it, which covers nearly every planet, moon and asteroid:\n\nusing AstroEpochs, AstroUniverse\n\nstruct MySpin <: AbstractOrientationModel\n    α₀::Float64          # rad\n    δ₀::Float64          # rad\n    W₀::Float64          # rad at J2000\n    spin_rate::Float64   # rad/day\nend\n\nfunction AstroUniverse.body_axes_rotation(m::MySpin, naifid, t::Time)\n    d = t.tdb.jd - 2451545.0\n    W = m.W₀ + m.spin_rate * d\n    return pole_axes_rotation(m.α₀, m.δ₀, W, 0.0, 0.0, m.spin_rate / 86400)\nend\n\nReturns\n\nA 6×6 matrix that rotates a state, position stacked on velocity, from inertial axes into the body's rotating axes. Position is in the top three rows and velocity in the bottom three, with the ω × r term of the rotating axes already included.\n\nNotes\n\nThe rotation is R_z(W) · R_x(π/2 − δ₀) · R_z(π/2 + α₀), the convention of the IAU working group on cartographic coordinates and rotational elements (Archinal et al., Celestial Mechanics and Dynamical Astronomy 130:22, 2018, §2.1). Row 3 of the rotation is the body's north pole in inertial axes.\n\nDifferentiable in all six arguments, so an estimated pole carries derivative information through the rotation.\n\nAngles are not reduced to a range; W may be many turns' worth and the trigonometry handles it.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse, LinearAlgebra\n\njulia> M = pole_axes_rotation(deg2rad(317.68), deg2rad(52.89), 1.0, 0.0, 0.0, 7.088e-5);\n\njulia> size(M)\n(6, 6)\n\njulia> round(det(M[1:3, 1:3]), digits = 12)   # a proper rotation\n1.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.pole_axes_rotation"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.set_eop!-Tuple{SatelliteToolboxTransformations.EopIau1980}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"set_eop!(table) -> table\n\nInstall an EOP table, an EopIau1980 or EopIau2000A, for the current Julia session.\n\nThe table's type determines which series is replaced, and the other is unchanged. Installing a table does not read files or use the network.\n\nFor repeatable EOP results, install saved tables or load local files with eop_load before evaluating transformations, for every series your calculation uses. SPICE kernels are managed separately and must also be in the cache for a run with no network.\n\nReturns\n\nThe installed table.\n\nExample\n\nusing AstroUniverse\n\ntable = eop(IAU2006())\nset_eop!(table)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.set_eop!"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.set_eop_corrections!-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"set_eop_corrections!(models) -> Vector{AbstractEopCorrection}\n\nInstall the corrections added to the daily EOP table for this session, replacing any installed before. An empty vector removes them all.\n\nThe corrections change every Earth-fixed result: frame transformations, ground station states and the orientation of Earth gravity. Record the installed corrections with your results so the calculation can be reproduced.\n\nReturns\n\nThe installed vector.\n\nExample\n\nusing EpicycleEnterprise                 # provides IersSubdaily\n\nset_eop_corrections!([IersSubdaily()])\nset_eop_corrections!([])                 # the table alone\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.set_eop_corrections!"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.set_frame_theory!-Tuple{AbstractFrameTheory}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"set_frame_theory!(theory::AbstractFrameTheory) -> theory\n\nSet the Earth precession-nutation theory for this session.\n\nChanging it changes computed results, because the two theories are different physical models. Record the selected theory with your results so the calculation can be reproduced.\n\nThe theory is Earth's orientation model, so set_orientation!(earth, theory) is the same call. It governs Earth frames, the default EOP series, and Earth gravity built after it is set.\n\nReturns\n\nThe installed theory.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse\n\njulia> set_frame_theory!(IAU2006())\nIAU2006()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.set_frame_theory!"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.set_gravparam!-Tuple{CelestialBody, Real}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"set_gravparam!(body, μ)\n\nSet the body’s gravitational parameter μ, in km³/s². The value must be finite and positive. The body passed in is changed, so a built-in body changes for the rest of the session.\n\nReturns\n\nThe modified body.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse\n\njulia> body = CelestialBody(\"Test\", 1.0, 1.0, 0.0, 1000001);\n\njulia> set_gravparam!(body, 2.0) === body\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.set_gravparam!"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.set_orientation!-Tuple{Any, AbstractOrientationModel}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"set_orientation!(body, model)\n\nSet the orientation model of a body, by its NAIF ID, for the current Julia session.\n\nArguments\n\nbody — a body, or a NAIF ID.\nmodel — an AbstractOrientationModel.\n\nReturns\n\nThe model, so a registration can be written inline.\n\nNotes\n\nReplaces whatever the body used before, including the built-in default. Every reference to that body sees the new model.\n\nAn included model is checked against the body, and a mismatch raises an ArgumentError. A custom model is accepted without being evaluated, and a SPICE frame is checked when the rotation is evaluated.\n\nA body with no included model has no body-fixed axes until a model is set, and asking for them raises an error.\n\nEarth's orientation is the frame theory, so for Earth this is set_frame_theory! under another name: set_orientation!(earth, FK5()) and set_frame_theory!(FK5()) are the same call, and change Earth frames, the EOP series and gravity together. Earth accepts only a frame theory, since a pole-and-meridian model in its place would silently replace the ITRF chain.\n\nExample\n\nusing AstroUniverse\n\nset_orientation!(moon, LunarME())      # the Moon's mean-Earth axes\nset_orientation!(earth, FK5())         # select the FK5 Earth frame theory\n\nFor a custom model, see AbstractOrientationModel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.set_orientation!"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.set_orientation_parameters!-Tuple{Any, NamedTuple}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"set_orientation_parameters!(body, nt::NamedTuple)\n\nUpdate the named parameters of a body's orientation model.\n\nArguments\n\nbody — a body, or a NAIF ID.\nnt::NamedTuple — parameter names and their new values. Names not given keep their current values.\n\nReturns\n\nThe updated model.\n\nNotes\n\nEvery reference to the body sees the new values afterward. Estimation code writes a solution back this way, and this package does not track which parameters are being solved for.\n\nRaises if the body has no orientation model, or if a name is not one of its parameters.\n\nExample\n\nusing AstroUniverse\n\nstruct PoleModel <: AbstractOrientationModel\n    pole_ra::Float64     # deg\nend\n\nbennu = CelestialBody(\"Bennu\", 4.892e-9, 0.2825, 0.0, 2101955)\nset_orientation!(bennu, PoleModel(85.46))\n\norientation_parameters(bennu)                          # what can be written\nset_orientation_parameters!(bennu, (; pole_ra = 85.51))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.set_orientation_parameters!"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.set_orientation_parameters-Union{Tuple{M}, Tuple{M, NamedTuple}} where M<:AbstractOrientationModel","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"set_orientation_parameters(model, nt::NamedTuple) -> model\n\nReturn a new model with the named parameters replaced. Names not given keep their current values.\n\nArguments\n\nmodel — the model to copy.\nnt::NamedTuple — parameter names and their new values. Every name must be one the model has; an unknown name raises rather than being ignored.\n\nReturns\n\nA new model of the same kind, with those parameters replaced.\n\nNotes\n\nNumeric fields are promoted to a common type when needed to rebuild the model, so writing one estimated value works on a model whose fields share one numeric type.\n\nTo write onto a body rather than a model, use set_orientation_parameters!.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse\n\njulia> struct Spin2 <: AbstractOrientationModel\n           pole_ra::Float64\n           pole_dec::Float64\n       end\n\njulia> orientation_parameters(set_orientation_parameters(Spin2(85.46, -60.36), (; pole_ra = 85.5)))\n(pole_ra = 85.5, pole_dec = -60.36)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.set_orientation_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.translate-Tuple{CelestialBody, CelestialBody, Time}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"translate(from::CelestialBody, to::CelestialBody, t::Time) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nReturn the geometric position of to relative to from at the epoch t, in ICRF axes. No light-time or aberration correction is applied.\n\nArguments\n\nfrom: Observing/origin body.\nto: Target body.\nt: Epoch, in any time scale. The ephemeris is read in TDB, using both parts of the date.\n\nReturns\n\nThe 3-element position [x, y, z], in km.\n\nNotes\n\nSPICE labels this frame \"J2000\"; it is ICRF-aligned and is not the FK5 mean equator and equinox of J2000.\n\nThe default kernels are loaded on first use, downloading any that are missing. A date outside the loaded kernels' coverage raises an error from SPICE, and a Time that carries derivatives raises an error, since SPICE is not differentiable.\n\nSafe to call from threads, and the SPICE queries run one at a time. A loop over many bodies at one epoch can convert the epoch once and pass t.tdb.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse, AstroEpochs\n\njulia> t = Time(2458018.0, TDB(), JD());\n\njulia> round.(translate(earth, moon, t); digits = 3)\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n -375694.599\n  -96115.682\n  -12226.883\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.translate"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.translate_state-Tuple{CelestialBody, CelestialBody, Time}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"translate_state(from::CelestialBody, to::CelestialBody, t::Time) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nReturn the geometric position and velocity of to relative to from at the epoch t, in ICRF axes. No light-time or aberration correction is applied.\n\nArguments\n\nfrom: Observing/origin body.\nto: Target body.\nt: Epoch, in any time scale. The ephemeris is read in TDB, using both parts of the date.\n\nReturns\n\n6-element state [x, y, z, vx, vy, vz] in km and km/s, from from to to.\n\nNotes\n\nSPICE labels this frame \"J2000\", which is ICRF-aligned and is not the FK5 mean equator and equinox of J2000. The two differ by the ~23 mas frame bias.\nKernel loading, coverage errors, threading, and differentiability are as for translate.\n\nExamples\n\nusing AstroUniverse, AstroEpochs\ns_em = translate_state(earth, moon, Time(2458018.0, TDB(), JD()))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.translate_state"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.unload_all_spice_kernels-Tuple{}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"unload_all_spice_kernels()\n\nUnload every SPICE kernel and turn off automatic loading of the default kernels for the rest of the session.\n\nAfterward, the kernels you download and load are the only ones in use. The files remain in the cache.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing after the SPICE kernel pool is cleared.\n\nExamples\n\nusing AstroUniverse\n\n# Create custom configuration\nunload_all_spice_kernels()\nloaded_spice_kernels()  # Empty\n\nSee also: load_spice_kernel, unload_spice_kernel\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.unload_all_spice_kernels"},{"category":"method","location":"#AstroUniverse.unload_spice_kernel-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"unload_spice_kernel(filename::AbstractString)\n\nUnload a SPICE kernel from the kernel pool.\n\nEvery loaded instance with this file name is removed. The file remains in the cache.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing after every loaded instance is removed.\n\nArguments\n\nfilename: Name of the kernel file to unload (e.g., \"de440s.bsp\")\n\nExamples\n\nusing AstroUniverse\n\n# Unload a kernel and load it again\nunload_spice_kernel(\"de440s.bsp\")\nload_spice_kernel(\"de440s.bsp\")\n\nSee also: load_spice_kernel, unload_all_spice_kernels\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.unload_spice_kernel"},{"category":"constant","location":"#AstroUniverse.KERNEL_SOURCES","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"KERNEL_SOURCES\n\nThe registered SPICE kernel sources, with their download URLs, checksums, sizes, and descriptions.\n\nAll but one are NAIF files served unchanged by JPL. A NAIF file name carries its version, so a pinned checksum stays valid.\n\nThe exception is epicycle_de440_1950-2100.bsp, which Gen Astro builds and hosts. It is DE440 for the whole solar system over 1950 to 2100, with both the barycenter and the center of every planet. Every CelestialBody names a body center, and JPL's de440.bsp has no planet center beyond Earth, so with that kernel alone translate(earth, mars, t) raises an error.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse\n\njulia> first(KERNEL_SOURCES).filename\n\"naif0012.tls\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.KERNEL_SOURCES"},{"category":"constant","location":"#AstroUniverse.earth","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"The built-in Earth (NAIF ID 399), with the WGS84 constants.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse; earth.naifid\n399\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.earth"},{"category":"constant","location":"#AstroUniverse.jupiter","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"The built-in Jupiter (NAIF ID 599). Its gravitational parameter includes its moons.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse; jupiter.naifid\n599\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.jupiter"},{"category":"constant","location":"#AstroUniverse.mars","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"The built-in Mars (NAIF ID 499). Its gravitational parameter includes its moons.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse; mars.naifid\n499\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.mars"},{"category":"constant","location":"#AstroUniverse.mercury","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"The built-in Mercury (NAIF ID 199).\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse; mercury.naifid\n199\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.mercury"},{"category":"constant","location":"#AstroUniverse.moon","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"The built-in Moon (NAIF ID 301).\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse; moon.naifid\n301\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.moon"},{"category":"constant","location":"#AstroUniverse.neptune","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"The built-in Neptune (NAIF ID 899). Its gravitational parameter includes its moons.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse; neptune.naifid\n899\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.neptune"},{"category":"constant","location":"#AstroUniverse.pluto","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"The built-in Pluto (NAIF ID 999). Its gravitational parameter includes its moons.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse; pluto.naifid\n999\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.pluto"},{"category":"constant","location":"#AstroUniverse.saturn","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"The built-in Saturn (NAIF ID 699). Its gravitational parameter includes its moons.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse; saturn.naifid\n699\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.saturn"},{"category":"constant","location":"#AstroUniverse.sun","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"The built-in Sun (NAIF ID 10).\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse; sun.naifid\n10\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.sun"},{"category":"constant","location":"#AstroUniverse.uranus","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"The built-in Uranus (NAIF ID 799). Its gravitational parameter includes its moons.\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse; uranus.naifid\n799\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.uranus"},{"category":"constant","location":"#AstroUniverse.venus","page":"AstroUniverse","text":"The built-in Venus (NAIF ID 299).\n\nExample\n\njulia> using AstroUniverse; venus.naifid\n299\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AstroUniverse.venus"}]
